        "I used a different encoding for each kind of categorical column. `CentralAir` has only two values, so I mapped it to 1/0. `ExterQual` and `KitchenQual` have a natural order (Poor < Fair < Typical < Good < Excellent), so I mapped them to 1–5, which keeps that order and uses one column each instead of many. `Neighborhood` and `BldgType` have no natural order, so I used one-hot encoding (`pd.get_dummies`, with `drop_first=True` to avoid redundant columns), because numbering them would wrongly imply that one neighbourhood is \"greater\" than another. A few missing values (e.g. in the test file) were filled with the training-set median (numeric) or the most common quality value (\"TA\")."
